          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The phrase denotes death-associated darkness: mortal peril, profound suffering, dying, or the realm of death, with a common spiritual extension to sin, alienation, bondage, and ignorance dispelled by God or Christ.\",\"book_summary\":\"The book treats the Shadow of Death as a contrary moral-spiritual pattern of sin, darkness, and bondage that leads ultimately to the grave, defined as the inverse of the life-giving Shadow of Christ.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Deathly darkness or a condition under the threat and power of death, including its common spiritual application to sinful alienation and bondage.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The sinful pattern of darkness and bondage whose course and wages culminate in death, opposed to Christ's life-giving pattern.\",\"core_relation\":\"BOOK_NARROWS\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUBSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The consensus covers literal mortal peril, severe affliction, dying, deep gloom, and Sheol as well as spiritual darkness. The book privileges the spiritual-moral strand and apparently excludes merely physical danger, natural gloom, or dying as sufficient instances unless they belong to the contrary pattern of sin and bondage. It also recasts death primarily as the pattern's destination or consequence rather than always the phrase's immediate referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no direct contradiction: the consensus explicitly recognizes sin, spiritual darkness, and bondage as common applications. The disagreement concerns which strand is definitive and whether the phrase names deathly danger itself or a sinful pattern that produces death.\",\"rationale\":\"The book's identification is recognizable within the consensus's common spiritual-darkness interpretation, so it is neither novel nor a replacement by an incompatible referent. It nevertheless materially narrows and systematizes that interpretation by making the phrase a technical opposite of the Shadow of Christ and identifying it with a morally contrary way of life culminating in death. This excludes several ordinary consensus cases and therefore exceeds a mere wording difference.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Psalm 107, Isaiah 9, Luke 1, and Romans 6 support an association among darkness, bondage, sin, death, and deliverance, but the printed texts do not clearly establish that “Shadow of Death” is a unified contrary pattern, the formal inverse of a “Shadow of Christ,” or that Christ's shadow is defined by appointed times, feasts, and assemblies. Psalm 23 also naturally supports mortal peril without identifying that peril as a sinful way of life.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
